So a lot of the scull code
doesn't match the book.

15
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:59,580
I've actually taken the liberty of
changing a lot of it to use mutex instead

16
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:02,740
of semaphore,
where mutex made the most sense.

17
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:05,850
But the concepts are very similar and
really want you to

18
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:10,111
understand semaphore mutex if anything
is easier to understand, because

19
00:01:10,111 --> 00:01:14,530
you're just dealing with one process or
one specific threat having access.

20
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:19,542
So a lot of the same kind of functions
that are available that we discussed for

21
00:01:19,542 --> 00:01:22,535
semaphores are going to
be available to mutex.

22
00:01:22,535 --> 00:01:26,712
And you're so
you'll have lock and try lock and

23
00:01:26,712 --> 00:01:31,100
there's also a nested
lock that's available for

24
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:36,429
obtaining the lock from multiple
nested function calls that

25
00:01:36,429 --> 00:01:41,774
we won't really get into a lot
of detail with in this course.

26
00:01:41,774 --> 00:01:46,540
But you also have the mutex_unlock and
the check to see if mutex is locked.

27
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:51,351
And then the interruptible variants that
we talked about with semaphores as well.

28
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:57,795
So how does mutex map to the semaphore
class that we talked about before?

29
00:01:57,795 --> 00:01:59,740
Mutex lock is essentially down.

30
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:03,360
Mutex trylock is down_trylock.

31
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:06,100
Mutex_lock_interruptible is
down_interruptible.

32
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:08,174
And then mutex_unlock would be up.

33
00:02:08,174 --> 00:02:11,558
And then the nested one is used for
the multiple locks and

34
00:02:11,558 --> 00:02:14,151
ordering between nested function calls.

35
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,511
So the mutex use in scull.

36
00:02:20,511 --> 00:02:22,244
We talked before about for

37
00:02:22,244 --> 00:02:26,271
semaphores that you had two
different ways to declare them.

38
00:02:26,271 --> 00:02:30,320
You could statically defined with
something like this would define mutex or

39
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:33,120
you could dynamically
defined with the mutex in it.

40
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:37,213
So in the scull case where we have
a structure member as a part of

41
00:02:37,213 --> 00:02:41,387
the structure,
we'd use mutex in it then at runtime to

42
00:02:41,387 --> 00:02:44,651
to initialize the lock
member of the structure.

43
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:53,111
So the also as you mentioned before,
the important thing about initializing

44
00:02:53,111 --> 00:02:57,500
the mutex is that it must be done
before we tell the Kernel about it.

45
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:02,031
So before we call scull_setup_cdev which
is going to ultimately call cdev_add,

46
00:03:02,031 --> 00:03:05,644
we need to make sure that we've
done our mutex initialization for

47
00:03:05,644 --> 00:03:08,796
the part of the structure,
because we have to make sure no

48
00:03:08,796 --> 00:03:13,017
object can be made available to the Kernel
before it can function properly.

49
00:03:13,017 --> 00:03:17,580
And before we call mutex in it, that
mutex is not going to function properly.

50
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:22,159
So we have to know when we're
notifying the Kernel and

51
00:03:22,159 --> 00:03:27,371
we have to make sure that our
mutex in it happens before that.

52
00:03:27,371 --> 00:03:31,462
So during module in it, we're going to
do both of those things and we can see

53
00:03:31,462 --> 00:03:35,621
the code here and scull_setup_cdev
that will initialize the device and

54
00:03:35,621 --> 00:03:39,061
we just need to make sure our
mutex in it happens before that.

55
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:44,850
So we talked about that most of the time
you're going to want to use interruptible.

56
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:47,885
And the fact that
interruptible could fail, for

57
00:03:47,885 --> 00:03:51,512
instance if a process is interacting and
you hit Ctrl+C or

58
00:03:51,512 --> 00:03:56,040
send a signal to the process
that you wanted to terminate.

59
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:58,140
So how would we handle that case?

60
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:03,575
And the book talks about some suggestions
here in terms of what to return

61
00:04:03,575 --> 00:04:08,661
to user space when you get interrupted
attempting to lock a mutex.

62
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:14,859
In some cases it could be that if
there was a reason to continue,

63
00:04:14,859 --> 00:04:18,934
if you didn't want to handle
the signal by exiting,

64
00:04:18,934 --> 00:04:23,320
the best thing to do would
be to restart the operation.

65
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:28,230
In that case you can use -ERESTARTSYS as
an error that's defined to let the user

66
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:30,367
space know that that's the case.

67
00:04:30,367 --> 00:04:35,245
Then it could also be the case that for
instance you started some operation and

68
00:04:35,245 --> 00:04:38,411
you don't have a good way
to undo that operation.

69
00:04:38,411 --> 00:04:41,491
Like especially if you
are working with the device,

70
00:04:41,491 --> 00:04:45,621
and in that case you might want to
return -EINTR to notify the user space

71
00:04:45,621 --> 00:04:49,961
that the operation was interrupted and
you might not be able to undo it, so

72
00:04:49,961 --> 00:04:53,740
the state of the device
might be inconsistent.

73
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:57,008
And then as we mentioned before, you
want to make sure that you're balancing

74
00:04:57,008 --> 00:04:59,371
every lock with an unlock,
including in error cases.

75
00:04:59,371 --> 00:05:02,283
So if you were obtaining multiple locks,
you obtained one and

76
00:05:02,283 --> 00:05:04,188
you were interrupted on the second one,

77
00:05:04,188 --> 00:05:07,561
you need to make sure that you release
that first one before you return.

78
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:14,844
So the book also discusses in addition to
just normal semaphores that the Kernel

79
00:05:14,844 --> 00:05:18,593
also supports that's called
Reader/Writer semaphores.

80
00:05:18,593 --> 00:05:22,508
And to think about why you'd want
a different type of semaphore than just

81
00:05:22,508 --> 00:05:24,840
a standard semaphore or mutex.

82
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:29,289
Think about access to a shared
resource by threads that are reading

83
00:05:29,289 --> 00:05:31,610
versus threads that are writing.

84
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:34,745
So if you're thinking about only
threads that are reading and

85
00:05:34,745 --> 00:05:37,771
you're thinking about accessing
a non atomic variables.

86
00:05:37,771 --> 00:05:42,740
So like maybe one a 64 Bit variable that
you're accessing on a 32 Bit system.

87
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:47,554
How many readers could concurrently
access that variable when

88
00:05:47,554 --> 00:05:50,470
rights to that variable are blocked?

89
00:05:50,470 --> 00:05:51,880
And the answer is infinite, right?

90
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:54,465
Because as long as we know
that the value isn't changing,

91
00:05:54,465 --> 00:05:56,440
you could let any number
of readers read it.

92
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:59,502
It's okay if they're not synchronized and
they're not reading at the same time,

93
00:05:59,502 --> 00:06:00,651
as long as it's not changing.

94
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:06,497
But if you turn that around and you say,
okay, what if a right was occurring,

95
00:06:06,497 --> 00:06:08,330
how many readers could access?

96
00:06:08,330 --> 00:06:12,977
Now the answer is 0, it's not safe to let
anyone access it when you're in the middle

97
00:06:12,977 --> 00:06:15,740
of writing it if it's not a ton.

98
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:19,134
So to answer the question
about would every thread need

99
00:06:19,134 --> 00:06:22,040
an exclusive lock to access a variable?

100
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:26,331
The answer is technically no,
if most threads are only reading it,

101
00:06:26,331 --> 00:06:29,970
they really wouldn't need
exclusive access to read it.

102
00:06:29,970 --> 00:06:33,540
It would only be the writer thread
that needs exclusive access.

103
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:35,454
And as long as you're blocking writers,

104
00:06:35,454 --> 00:06:37,880
an infinite number of reader
threads could access.

105
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:42,138
So this is really the point of
a read/writers semaphore is to kind of

106
00:06:42,138 --> 00:06:46,561
optimize that case if you have
a variable that fits that description.

107
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:50,270
So you're very similar functions for

108
00:06:50,270 --> 00:06:54,340
using reader/writer semaphores
in terms of in it down up.

109
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:58,987
There's one thing to note here that
the trylock functions return 1

110
00:06:58,987 --> 00:07:02,751
instead of 0 on success,
which is kind of backwards.

111
00:07:02,751 --> 00:07:06,454
So that's something to pay
attention if you use that.

112
00:07:06,454 --> 00:07:11,860
There's also a mechanism to convert a lock
from a write lock into a read lock.

113
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:20,261
Because again, the writer is the case
that needs to block every other access.

114
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:24,190
So if you've finished writing to the lock,
but you still want to be able to read

115
00:07:24,190 --> 00:07:27,711
the lock, you're able to just
downgrade from a write to a read lock.

116
00:07:27,711 --> 00:07:31,547
Writers get priority, so if there's
a writer waiting, it's going to block any

117
00:07:31,547 --> 00:07:35,161
other readers from happening until
the writer gets a chance to complete.

118
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:40,077
And the time when you want to use
this would be when write access is

119
00:07:40,077 --> 00:07:44,580
rarely required and you only need write
access fairly briefly when you need it.

120
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:46,996
So this doesn't make sense for
every situation, but

121
00:07:46,996 --> 00:07:48,561
it's good to know that it exists.

122
00:07:50,140 --> 00:07:53,773
Another variant on the locking
schemes that we've discussed so

123
00:07:53,773 --> 00:07:55,061
far are completions.

124
00:07:56,140 --> 00:08:00,276
So completions are designed as you
probably guessed from the name when you

125
00:08:00,276 --> 00:08:02,753
want to wait for
some activity to complete.

126
00:08:02,753 --> 00:08:06,663
And you might say, well, couldn't I
just use a semaphore for this and

127
00:08:06,663 --> 00:08:09,240
the answer is generally yes you could.

128
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:13,916
However, the book talks about
the fact that the software design of

129
00:08:13,916 --> 00:08:17,860
semaphore is really optimized for
the available case.

130
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:22,262
So it's optimized to make, if the
semaphore is available, that's going to be

131
00:08:22,262 --> 00:08:26,667
the fastest code path, because if you
think about access to a critical region

132
00:08:26,667 --> 00:08:31,580
for instance, it's probably just very
small windows of time that that's blocked.

133
00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:34,492
So you want to make semaphore
optimize such that for

134
00:08:34,492 --> 00:08:38,719
the high percentage of the time that
you don't need to block the thread,

135
00:08:38,719 --> 00:08:42,539
it's a very small overhead
to use the semaphore.

136
00:08:42,539 --> 00:08:46,437
By contrast, the completions
are designed for the opposite.

137
00:08:46,437 --> 00:08:51,109
Completions are designed for the case
where typically the, when you check it,

138
00:08:51,109 --> 00:08:53,140
the completion is not available.

139
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:56,760
So you've kicked off some thread and
you're going to be checking periodically

140
00:08:56,760 --> 00:09:00,045
for the thread to be complete and
that amount of time that that threat is

141
00:09:00,045 --> 00:09:03,440
going to run and the completion is not
going to be complete is large relative to

142
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:07,340
the number of times that you're
going to check it potentially.

143
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:11,972
So the example when this would be a case
would be if you just started a thread and

144
00:09:11,972 --> 00:09:14,753
you're going to want to wait for
it to complete and

145
00:09:14,753 --> 00:09:18,051
that's a relatively long
amount of time that occurs.

146
00:09:19,220 --> 00:09:24,476
There's also the complete/complete_all
functions which support use and

147
00:09:24,476 --> 00:09:26,021
interrupt handlers.

148
00:09:26,021 --> 00:09:30,586
So you can set up a completion and
initialize await in a process and

149
00:09:30,586 --> 00:09:34,736
then an interrupt could come through and
issue a complete or

150
00:09:34,736 --> 00:09:38,981
complete_all that would complete
your wait in user space.

151
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:43,943
The book gives some suggestions
about when to use completions and

152
00:09:43,943 --> 00:09:46,345
it said if you're thinking
about using yield() or

153
00:09:46,345 --> 00:09:49,320
some kind of m(1)sleep loop to
allow something to complete.

154
00:09:49,320 --> 00:09:52,780
Its probably an example of something
that completions was designed for.

155
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:56,240
So you should be looking at
using a completion instead.

156
00:09:56,240 --> 00:10:00,635
The way you use the completion is similar
to the way we've talked about with mutexs

157
00:10:00,635 --> 00:10:02,046
and semaphores already.

158
00:10:02,046 --> 00:10:05,992
You can have these declare macros that
you used to declare it globally or

159
00:10:05,992 --> 00:10:09,696
there's also a declare that you can
use to declare it on the stack.

160
00:10:09,696 --> 00:10:12,124
And then you can also
use an in it like this,

161
00:10:12,124 --> 00:10:15,573
if you're initializing it as
part of a structure variable.

162
00:10:15,573 --> 00:10:21,386
So the general way you'd use it, this
is an example that's shown in the book,

163
00:10:21,386 --> 00:10:27,379
you initialize the completion, you define
it and initialize the completion and

164
00:10:27,379 --> 00:10:32,044
then you pass it to some work that
some work function that will be

165
00:10:32,044 --> 00:10:36,399
running in a different thread,
maybe on a different CPU.

166
00:10:36,399 --> 00:10:43,540
And then you call wait for completion and
wait for the completion to be signaled.

167
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:48,049
So on that other thread that's doing
work or maybe it's an interrupt

168
00:10:48,049 --> 00:10:53,024
whenever it's done, you would call
complete on the value that was passed,

169
00:10:53,024 --> 00:10:58,030
and that would trigger the wait for
completion to continue on the first CPU.

170
00:10:58,030 --> 00:11:02,861
So it's a way to communicate
between two different CPU threads.

171
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:09,379
There's a similar variants here as to
what we've already seen with mutex,

172
00:11:09,379 --> 00:11:13,340
where you have the interruptible
versions that can be interrupted.

173
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:17,765
You also have time out versions where you
can tell it this is the maximum amount of

174
00:11:17,765 --> 00:11:21,331
time I want to wait for it and
if it doesn't complete by this time,

175
00:11:21,331 --> 00:11:22,880
return and do some action.

176
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:27,452
And then you can look at the return value
to figure out which of these states

177
00:11:27,452 --> 00:11:28,207
occurred.

178
00:11:28,207 --> 00:11:32,704
Then the complete and
complete_all just tell you

179
00:11:32,704 --> 00:11:37,953
the difference between, and
should I signal complete for

180
00:11:37,953 --> 00:11:43,120
the most recent thread that's waiting for
completion?

181
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:46,290
Or should I signal complete for
any thread that's waiting for completion?

182
00:11:46,290 --> 00:11:49,669
And the last is for
any thread that's waiting for

183
00:11:49,669 --> 00:11:53,340
completion is what complete_all does.

184
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:57,579
And one other important thing to note
about complete_all is that it would signal

185
00:11:57,579 --> 00:11:59,711
all the current and the future waiters.

186
00:11:59,711 --> 00:12:02,534
So if you might have a thread
that would start and

187
00:12:02,534 --> 00:12:07,196
call complete all before you actually
started waiting from the other thread,

188
00:12:07,196 --> 00:12:11,250
your thread that calls weight is
going to just complete immediately.

189
00:12:11,250 --> 00:12:12,998
And so
if that's the behavior that you want,

190
00:12:12,998 --> 00:12:14,753
that would be a reason
to use complete_all.